  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2007 CSH Protocols article has a very quiet scholarly record so far, but it does show one documented downstream reuse in Europe PMC and one indexed instance in OpenAIRE. The article is also linked to dataset citation metadata, suggesting it has been registered in a way that supports reuse tracking, even though the citation count itself remains at zero.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Imaging Actin in Tissue Slices from Transgenic Mouse Brain: Figure 1. is a protocol-oriented article in Cold Spring Harbor Protocols by Andrew Matus, Virginie Biou, Heike Brinkhaus, and Martijn Roelandse. Its subject profile centers on transgenic mouse brain tissue, actin imaging, and cell-biological methods, placing it firmly in the experimental neuroscience and microscopy toolkit rather than in a conceptual or clinical niche.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest evidence of uptake is modest but concrete: the record shows one downstream reuse, captured as a biomedical article mention in Europe PMC. That kind of signal indicates the work has been noticed and reused in the biomedical literature, even though the citation record in OpenAlex currently remains at zero.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The broader impact picture is therefore one of limited but traceable reach. OpenAIRE lists a single indexed instance for the article, and Crossref\u2019s data-citation services report successful handling of dataset-citation metadata for this work. Together, these signals suggest the article is discoverable and machine-readable in ways that support reuse, but the available record does not yet show a substantial citation trail.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There is also no reported public attention in the available event-data record, and scite does not show citation agreement or disagreement signals for this item. That makes the paper\u2019s footprint relatively narrow in the current sources, with its value appearing mainly in methodological reuse rather than in visible discussion or citation volume.</p>",
